Part 2 (optional, DONE): the all-zero NEW tip really is implemented — the thunk runs
_oid_hex_if_nonzero on BOTH tips (Remote.pm:523-524, helper at :546-551), so a zero
new tip yields to => undef. Added as a sibling call in the same subtest:

  $closure->( "refs/remotes/origin/gone", $old, $zero, 0 )  # returns 0
  -> { ref => .../gone, from => $HEX_OLD, to => undef, reason => "" }

Pinned as MEANING, not just return value: three assertions rather than one.
(a) the three-entry deep compare shows the two sentinels side by side — zero OLD
    tip => from undef ("did not exist yet"), zero NEW tip => to undef ("deleted");
(b) an explicit [ map { $_->{to} } @updated ] check that the deleted ref is undef
    and NOT the 40-zero oid — that is the distinction the normalisation exists for,
    and it would fail loudly if the normalisation were dropped from the b side only
    (oid_to_hex on zero bytes returns "0" x 40, which is not undef);
(c) reason is "" across all three, pinning the #16 unification at the thunk level.
